        "problem": "Find the third-degree Taylor polynomial ${P}_{3}(x)$ of $f(x)={e}^{2x}$ about $x=1.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n${P}_{3}(x)={e}^{2}\\,+$\n{{field-1}}\n$(x-1)\\,+$\n{{field-2}}\n$(x-1)^{2}\\,+$\n{{field-3}}\n$(x-1)^{3}$",
        "worked_solution": "To find the third-degree Taylor polynomial about $x=1,$ we plug $a=1$ into the general formula to give \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{3}(x)=f(1)+{f}^{\u2032}(1)(x-1)+\\frac{{f}^{\u2033}(1)}{2!}(x-1)^{2}+\\frac{{f}^{\u2034}(1)}{3!}(x-1)^{3} \\\\ {P}_{3}(x)=f(1)+{f}^{\u2032}(1)(x-1)+\\frac{{f}^{\u2033}(1)}{2}(x-1)^{2}+\\frac{{f}^{\u2034}(1)}{6}(x-1)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to compute $f(1),$ ${f}^{\u2032}(1),$ ${f}^{\u2033}(1),$ and ${f}^{\u2034}(1),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={e}^{2x} & \u27f9 &  & f(1) & ={e}^{2\\cdot 1}={e}^{2} \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(x) & =2{e}^{2x} & \u27f9 &  & {f}^{\u2032}(1) & =2{e}^{2\\cdot 1}=2{e}^{2} \\\\ {f}^{\u2033}(x) & =4{e}^{2x} & \u27f9 &  & {f}^{\u2033}(1) & =4{e}^{2\\cdot 1}=4{e}^{2} \\\\ {f}^{\u2034}(x) & =8{e}^{2x} & \u27f9 &  & {f}^{\u2034}(1) & =8{e}^{2\\cdot 1}=8{e}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we plug the results for $f(1),$ ${f}^{\u2032}(1),$ ${f}^{\u2033}(1),$ and ${f}^{\u2034}(1)$ into our third-degree Taylor polynomial. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{3}(x)={e}^{2}+2{e}^{2}(x-1)+2{e}^{2}(x-1)^{2}+\\frac{4{e}^{2}}{3}(x-1)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
